342:The \emph{degree} $\delta_G(v)$ of the vertex $v$ in $G$ is represented as the cardinality of the open neighborhood of $v$, that is $\delta_G(v)=|N_G(v)|$. The \emph{maximum degree} of a vertex in a graph $G$ is denoted by $\Delta(G)$. Clearly, $1\leq \Delta(G)\leq |V(G)|-1$ as we consider only simple graphs having no vertices of degree zero. A \emph{leaf} of $G$ is a vertex $v\in V(G)$ with degree $\delta_G(v)=1$ and in contrast, if $\delta_G(v)=|V(G)|-1$, then the vertex $v$ is called as \emph{universal vertex}. For the specific case of the direct product of two graphs $G$ and $H$, we recall that $\delta_{G\times H}(g,h)=\delta_G(g)\delta_H(h)$ and $\Delta(G\times H)=\Delta(G)\Delta(H)$ by (\ref{neighb}).
