Implemented a self-contained Track B generator based on Section 3, Theorem 10(iii) of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.13608).

Files:

- [gen_2005_13608.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.13608/gen_2005_13608.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.13608/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.13608/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2005.13608/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl) — error-only harness output

Local results:

- All G1–G9(c) gates pass.
- Guessing: 0/200,000.
- Exact valid density: \(56{,}454/476{,}169{,}905{,}664 \approx 1.19\times10^{-7}\).
- Five attacks: 0/8 each.
- Track B reference algorithm: 8/8, up to 1,273,143 word operations.
- Compact route: 284 operations.
- Answer: 25 characters, six atomic integers.
- Canonical-key invariance and doubled-size construction pass.

External validation remains blocked: three `harden.py` invocations received HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` before any scored oracle response. Therefore the main hardness verdict and the hinted/placebo G9 transcripts could not be produced without fabricating evidence. The README records this limitation explicitly. Restoring OpenRouter capacity is the only remaining step.
